error[E1010]: `fwd` may send `s` on `out`, and its bytes live in region `proc:worker`, the proc's own region, which is freed when the proc exits — before a receiver could ever use them
 --> mem:///main.lu:6:14
  |
4 | fn worker(n: int, out: channel[str]) -> !int {
  |    ------ `proc:worker` is a `spawn proc` entry: a proc owns its regions and they bulk-free at its exit, so nothing built here outlives it ([conc.proc.kill])
5 |     let s = "built in proc, {n} uses"
  |             ------------------------- allocated here, into region `proc:worker`
6 |     fwd(out, s)
  |         --- `out` is a channel that carries bytes: the callee may send any other argument on it
  |              ^ this argument's bytes are in region `proc:worker`
  |
  = note: a call may send what it is handed: a callee given a channel can send any other argument
    on it, or a value it builds in its caller's current region, and a payload must outlive
    the frame that sends it [conc.chan.payload] [mem.region.escape]. A proc's regions die
    with the proc ([conc.proc.kill] step 3): build the bytes in the SPAWNER and pass them in
    as a parameter, and send them from the proc entry itself — or send a payload that owns
    no region storage (a literal, an `int`, a `bool`).
